; A constant of a block stays a constant in the blocks inside it. Kurt's `is_var` only looked at
; the constants of the current level, so inside `assume %E` (within `let %E`) the assumption counted
; as "for all %E", and `Q` followed from it: closing both blocks gave `%E implies Q`, and with
; `%E := true`, `Q` from nothing (found by translating proofs to Lean, 2026-10-02).
;
; Since 2026-10-20 `let` fixes a term only (a statement variable needs none: an `assume` holds it
; fixed), so the statement case is checked without `let`, and the term case as before.
load propositional, equality, logic
prop Q
const d
expect "ProofError"
    assume %E
        Q
assume %E
    %E
%E implies %E
expect "TypeError" "`let` fixes a term"
    let %E
expect "ProofError"
    let x
        assume x = x
            x = d
